  - The only config value today is [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT = "https://api.example.com/login"[39m in [38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m.

  So there's no shared module system between the browser code and the Node code, and no build step. That makes "how does
  the settings module know which environment it's in" the fork that decides everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m with no bundler, the environment can only be selected at load time in the browser, at serve/deploy time by
  swapping a file, or by inspecting the page's own hostname at runtime. These differ in what you have to do to ship a
  new environment later.

  - [1mHostname-based switch inside the module[0m — one committed [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m maps [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m → endpoint. Buys: zero
    deploy machinery, works opening the file locally. Costs: the env list lives in source, so a new environment is a
    code change. Rules out per-deploy overrides without editing code. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mSeparate per-env config file, swapped at deploy[0m — [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m holds only the defaults; deploys overwrite it (or
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m points at [38;5;153mconfig.prod.js[39m). Buys: new environments need no code change. Costs: requires a deploy step that
    does the swap; untracked drift between envs. Moderate to undo.
  - [1mGlobal override hook[0m — [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m reads [38;5;153mwindow.APP_CONFIG[39m if present, else falls back to a baked-in default. Buys:
    both of the above (hardcode for dev, inject a [38;5;153m<script>[39m in prod). Costs: one more indirection, and the contract for
    that global becomes something you have to keep documented.

  [1mMy recommendation: the hostname-based switch[0m, because this repo has no deploy pipeline to hang a file swap on, and the
  override hook is machinery for a need that doesn't exist yet. It's also the cheapest of the three to upgrade later —
  the other two are strictly additions to it.
